// CAPTION, accepted with the build note: The people of Ephesus built this temple after the fire of 356 BC and paid for it themselves. Pliny wrote that its columns stood sixty feet high.
// The Temple of Artemis at Ephesus, late on a clear afternoon in late spring about 150 BC (design/sw/briefs/temple-of-artemis.json).
// The axes are the TEMPLE'S: +X from its front to its back ('east'), +Z to the right hand of one walking in ('south'),
// y 0 the forecourt's paving, x 0 the front row's column centres, the long axis on z 0. The temple itself is the part
// (src/parts/temple-of-artemis.js), added at the origin unturned. This file is the land, the light and the flight:
// the flat wet marsh with its stream, pools, reeds and willows, the gulf, the hills, the forecourt, the altar court,
// the boundary stones and offerings, the precinct's things, the day's people on the paving, the road with its fair,
// birds, smoke, the low sun with its one shadow map, the route, the looks, the pace and the ring.
// THE DAY (ours): late spring, a day of offerings; no feast is named and nothing claims the temple was built for this hour.
// THE FLIGHT (the owner's ruling of 9 Oct, 13:47): in from the north west, about 200 m in all: low
// over the golden reed of the stream's old arm, the temple whole and cornerwise ahead; onto the precinct's grass
// between the day's garlanded posts, altars and jars, the altar court with its long altar and its fires to the right; up over the corner of the
// steps round the corner column; south down the aisle between the two front rows; left into the porch and in by the great
// door; DOWN the stair and level over the old floor of the court toward the shrine 2.3 m over that floor; then one climb
// over the shrine's roof to a ring before the sunlit upper part of the court's east wall. That the temple's inner room was
// a court open to the sky is the probable reading, not a proven one.
// THE LIGHT: ONE light for the whole flight, late and deep gold: the sun ON THE TEMPLE'S AXIS in the west,
// 12.5 to 13 degrees up (the seed deals within that band), one sky light of one strength everywhere (nothing follows the
// route, the lens or the clock). The west front blazes; the great door's blade of sun lies down the old floor of the
// court and stands on the old shrine's front; the court's east wall is gold above and blue below. Every shade is blue and
// about four tenths as bright as the lit marble beside it; long shadows lie toward the temple from all that stands on the ground. The sun itself is never
// in the picture: no look turns west of the line from north to south (the least angle is counted in the report).
// Nothing is lettered. No statue of the goddess. No tree inside the boundary stones. No figure in the temple's court.
// ROUND r11, IN ONE LINE: THE BYTES ARE LIVE'S (world md5 372e25a8ccabb8c8a62d46bcf42b672b, part md5 7ebbbd3fb74fec7f933ffbbc297dcd1e) AND FROM ROUND r10u (world md5 931076f136dc07d50016e6f8d084483b, part md5 298f43712b482e8115e6c56a2c1c9108) CAME THREE THINGS BY PLACE AND NOTHING ELSE: THE ALTAR COURT as rebuilt and THE FORECOURT (its many-sided paving; the knots of people before the court's open side), both in this file, and THE FLOOR'S LIGHT UNDER THE ROOF, on the floor alone, in the temple part's file.
// Everything else here is live's: the route, the gaze and the pace, the light, the ring's light, the procession, the things left on the steps, the temple's dressing.
// THE AISLES' FLOOR CHANGES TOO (one floor, one law; it is the temple part's): the temple's floor under the roof, the aisles' with the porch's, takes r10u's light with its easing and its two companion lines, bounded to the floor's own kind and level; every other face under the roof keeps live's arithmetic term for term.
// PLACED BY TYPED PLACES OF THE WORLD (said here in the open; each at its own block below, none by a picture's place): the
// altar court as the research's plan has it, with ONE GROUP OF TWO PLAIN BRONZE FIGURES ON A PILLAR at its north west (THE SOURCE'S: a group of bronze figures on a pillar, one of them bare-footed and over 2 m high.
// OURS, WHOLE: that they are two, the second one's height, their pose, their dress (a plain short garment), that both go bare-footed, and the pillar's plan, height and place), THE INCENSE SELLERS' ROW north west of it and THE KNOTS OF PEOPLE before its open
// west side (ours; people of the fair who have come up, none added). THE GAZE OF THE WAY IN RESTS ON NONE OF THEM: it is as it was before the
// court was rebuilt, the temple whole its subject (the looks' block).
// NOTHING OF THEM IS BUILT, PLACED, HIDDEN, MOVED, SIZED, LIT OR TIMED BY WHERE THE EYE IS, BY A SEED'S NUMBER OR BY ANY MOMENT OF THE FLIGHT, but for the three laws by the eye named below: the seed deals them through streams of their own
// names ('incense-sellers', 'west-knots', 'altar-fire') or the old streams of their kind (the sellers' threads by the stream 'incense' after the porch's, those who attend in the court by 'altar-court', the court's garlands by 'garlands' and 'garland-leaves', the middle hearth's flames and the smoke by 'smoke'), and the fires, the torches' flames and the sellers' threads run closed courses on the world's clock.
// THE LAWS BY THE EYE THAT TOUCH THEM (section 153), each of live's own kind: (1) the new flames are drawn in the smoke's own mesh and the sellers' threads in the incense's own mesh and take those meshes' law as it stood (each thins at the lens over 0.6 to 2.5 m of true distance, as all the air's things here do);
// (2) THE ALTAR'S SMOKE KEEPS LIVE'S OWN GLOW TOWARD THE SUN BY THE DIRECTION OF SIGHT (a law of light by the eye, live's own code unchanged: the smoke is brighter where one looks through it toward the sun, and a little where one looks straight away from the sun; the flames do not read it); (3) THE PAVING'S VEINS AND JOINTS FADE OUT BY THE PIXEL'S FOOTPRINT on the ground (px): THREE NEW TERMS of live's own kind (the veins fade out between a footprint of 0.04 and 0.3 m; the joint's dark hair and the joint's lit edge between 0.05 and 0.5 m), as live's joints faded.
// INVENTED (the owner's word of 02:46 UTC on 12 Oct, section 172 c: it struck none), named here as round r10u named them, those of the three things. Each has its own block below. NONE OF THESE STANDS IN A CAPTION.
// INVENTED, OURS:
//   THE INCENSE SELLERS' TABLES, bowls, baskets and threads: the sale of incense in the sanctuary is sourced at second hand from a lease of early Hellenistic times; the stalls are not described.
//   THE KNOTS OF THE FAIR'S PEOPLE before the altar court's open side, with a basket bearer and LIT TORCHES: torches and baskets are carried in the procession of a novel of Roman date: no witness of the time.
//     Plain everyday torches, small in daylight, a flame and no glare: the hearths' own flame at TORCH_FIRE of its size; it casts no light and nothing is drawn round it (a flame's brightest colour, FLAME.body, has a
//     luminance under 0.8, and this world's bloom threshold is 1.05: the kit's bloom adds no halo to a flame). Carried only by the fair's people, each PEOPLE_OFF and more from the flown line by the law of his place (counted on forty seeds, r11: no carrier nearer than 29.0 m, no flame nearer than 30.7 m).
